WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.169
SHEARD: Yanis Varoufakis is on the faculty
of economic studies at the University of Athens,

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:08.980
and of course, most notably, I think for this
occasion, the former minister of finance of

00:00:08.980 --> 00:00:14.000
the Hellenic Republic, Greece.
And I think it's fair to say, Yanis, that

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:21.450
you had one of the most colorful and controversial
tours of duty as a finance minister in the

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:25.590
modern era. So what I'd like to do is open
up the conversation first of all by going

00:00:25.590 --> 00:00:32.340
back to that-those very fateful 10 days or
so in the last week of June and the first

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:38.720
week of July last year, which really did make
anybody who was paying attention-make their

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.720
head spin.
Just to frame this a little bit for the audience,

00:00:42.720 --> 00:00:50.080
to remind you, when Syriza had come in, I
think the program that was in place at the

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:54.300
time-that was in January-had about a month
to run. Then you had a four-month reprieve

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.940
through to the end of June. And then, just
a few days before that deadline loomed, I

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:07.300
guess the Eurogroup made a "take it or leave
it" offer to Greece and Mr. Tsipras surprised

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:11.570
the world by calling a referendum. Various
other things happened. I think the banks were

00:01:11.570 --> 00:01:16.140
closed, so it was this pretty hairy stuff.
For about, you know, seven or eight days later,

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:22.530
that referendum was held and there was a resounding
"no" to the Eurogroup proposal. But then,

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:28.160
that very night, things took a big turn, about
180-degree turn. Mr. Tsipras then announced

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.130
that he was going to essentially negotiate
with the Eurogroup with a view to accepting

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:36.280
some kind of package, and at that point you
resigned as the minister of finance. I think

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:38.840
I'm-correct me if I'm wrong-more or less have
that right.

00:01:38.840 --> 00:01:43.860
So I'd love to start by just getting your
view on this. What was going on in that week

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:49.170
or so and how should we understand the history
books, exactly what happened?

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:58.840
VAROUFAKIS: That 10-day period seemed to the
outside world as if it was the moment of truth.

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:06.090
It wasn't. Even before that 10-day period
had begun, my heart sunk. I knew that the

00:02:06.090 --> 00:02:13.250
cause was lost. It had been lost sometime
in March-April. I can explain why. And we

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:18.780
were going through the motions.
I was hoping that a strong referendum result

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:24.160
would energize my prime minister to pick up
where we had left off. And I can explain what

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:31.910
I mean by that. But in the end-well, the referendum
result itself was a major surprise to me.

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:40.870
My wife, Danae, who is here with us today,
can confirm that on the afternoon of the referendum

00:02:40.870 --> 00:02:50.770
I sat on my desk-on my desk-at my desk and
I penned my fifth-no, fourth letter of resignation,

00:02:50.770 --> 00:02:56.209
on the assumption that we had lost the referendum,
that the "yes" had won.

00:02:56.209 --> 00:03:02.480
And a few hours later I found out that the
incredible Greek people had voted 62 percent.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:06.910
Every single constituency in the land had
voted "no." And I just couldn't believe my

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:11.860
eyes. And I thought that that would hopefully
re-energize our team and the prime minister.

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:17.330
It didn't. I walked into this office. He said,
it's time to surrender, just when the Greek

00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:23.120
people had backed us. And I tried for three
hours to dissuade him. I failed, so I walked

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:30.319
out and resigned.
This opportunity to address you and to have

00:03:30.319 --> 00:03:38.200
this discussion with you is precious to me
because of the major disconnect between what

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:45.920
was going on and what was being reported by
the mainstream media-actually by all the media.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:53.370
Never before in my life had I imagined that
it was possible to have such a gap, a chasm

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:58.349
between reality and reported reality.
SHEARD: So would you like to amplify that

00:03:58.349 --> 00:04:03.810
and maybe also-you made some reference to
the game was essentially up in March or so

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:08.980
of last year but continued right through to
the dramatic events at the end of June, early

00:04:08.980 --> 00:04:12.629
July.
VAROUFAKIS: Allow me to just frame the discussion

00:04:12.629 --> 00:04:20.639
as succinctly as I can by saying that the
problem began, for argument's sake, maybe

00:04:20.639 --> 00:04:25.539
starting in 1821 with the Greek Revolution.
No, the problem began in 2010 when the Greek

00:04:25.539 --> 00:04:33.939
state went bankrupt-well and thoroughly, deeply,
irreversibly insolvent, and the great and

00:04:33.939 --> 00:04:42.520
the good in Europe-that included the Central
Bank, Chancellor Merkel, Wolfgang Sch�uble,

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:47.190
the president of the commission, the president
of the Euro Pool Group, the IMF, and the Greek

00:04:47.190 --> 00:04:54.879
government-decided to treat a case of irreversible
insolvency as a case of illiquidity, to pile

00:04:54.879 --> 00:05:01.319
up on the shoulders of the most bankrupt European
nation that lacked a central bank to have

00:05:01.319 --> 00:05:09.300
its back, the largest loan in history on conditions
of guarantee to shrink nominal GDP.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:13.289
And that would never end well. It was a typical
case of extending the crisis and pretending

00:05:13.289 --> 00:05:19.310
that it was solved. And of course eight months
later-nine months later, in 2011, 2012, there

00:05:19.310 --> 00:05:24.270
was a need for another "extend-and-pretend"
loan, and then another one, and the another

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:28.289
one.
And the only reason why I accepted the challenge

00:05:28.289 --> 00:05:32.010
of doing something that I had never imagined
I would ever do in my life, which is go into

00:05:32.010 --> 00:05:38.119
politics, to run for parliament, only because
the prime minister-the man who was going to

00:05:38.119 --> 00:05:43.789
be prime minister invited me to be his finance
minister-was because he and I had a simple

00:05:43.789 --> 00:05:49.569
agreement. I was not a member of the party,
so I was an outside within the party and within

00:05:49.569 --> 00:05:54.009
the Eurogroup, not a very enviable position
to be in.

00:05:54.009 --> 00:05:58.539
The agreement we had was very simple: We're
not going to take another loan from European

00:05:58.539 --> 00:06:04.749
taxpayers until and unless the Greek private
sector stabilized. It's really very simple:

00:06:04.749 --> 00:06:10.399
No more "extent and pretend," and we would
go down in flames rather than sign on the

00:06:10.399 --> 00:06:14.919
dotted line of another such agreement.
And the reason why I'm saying that the last

00:06:14.919 --> 00:06:22.110
10 days was neither here nor there really
was because from day one-well, actually, no,

00:06:22.110 --> 00:06:30.969
day three, I moved into the ministry on the
27th of January. Prior to that, Tsipras and

00:06:30.969 --> 00:06:41.199
I had agreed that the moment the troika lenders
threatens us with bank closures-because it

00:06:41.199 --> 00:06:46.089
was clear that that's what they were going
to do, and we had agreed on how to retaliate

00:06:46.089 --> 00:06:48.590
if they did this-
SHEARD: Because they could basically cut you

00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:54.219
off, cut the Greek banks and banking system
off from the euro system settlements.

00:06:54.219 --> 00:07:00.289
VAROUFAKIS: Well, the eurozone is a remarkable
set of institutions. We have a central bank

00:07:00.289 --> 00:07:05.149
without a state and states without a central
bank, so there's nothing easier than closing

00:07:05.149 --> 00:07:11.639
down the banking system of one of those member
states when you've got-especially after the

00:07:11.639 --> 00:07:18.939
deleveraging and the credit crunch following
2008, you have a death dance between insolvent

00:07:18.939 --> 00:07:27.919
states and insolvent nation state domiciled
banks. And if the Central Bank cuts liquidity

00:07:27.919 --> 00:07:33.610
off or signals an intention to think about
cutting liquidity off, it's finished.

00:07:33.610 --> 00:07:40.080
On the 3rd of December, 2014, more than a
month before our election, the Central Bank,

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:44.149
through its representative in Greece-the governor
of the Bank of Greece, which was of course

00:07:44.149 --> 00:07:49.340
part of the European system of central banks-did
something unique in the history of central

00:07:49.340 --> 00:07:57.599
banking globally. He came out with a statement
out of the blue signaling his concern about

00:07:57.599 --> 00:08:04.229
the liquidity of the Greek banks.
You know the story about the two men who meet

00:08:04.229 --> 00:08:09.879
at the golf club and one of them says, I made
my money by burning down my business and,

00:08:09.879 --> 00:08:15.819
you know, claiming the insurance. And the
other one says, well, I had a similar experience;

00:08:15.819 --> 00:08:20.009
mine was flooded. And the first man asked,
how did you start the flood? (Laughter.) In

00:08:20.009 --> 00:08:25.629
the case of, you know, the 3rd of December-
SHEARD: How do you start a run on the banking

00:08:25.629 --> 00:08:26.789
system?
VAROUFAKIS: Yeah. Well, it's very simple.

00:08:26.789 --> 00:08:33.470
If the Central Bank announces the possibility
of a liquidity crisis of the banks, that's

00:08:33.470 --> 00:08:37.750
it; it happens the next morning. So we had
a bank run-a slow-burning bank run before

00:08:37.750 --> 00:08:42.580
we came in. And we knew that this was going
to happen, and we knew as it happened.

00:08:42.580 --> 00:08:47.870
Thursday I was at the ministry. President
Dijsselbloem of the Eurogroup visited in my

00:08:47.870 --> 00:08:55.550
office. We had warm-up meetings with all the
aids and committees and all of that and then

00:08:55.550 --> 00:09:00.030
we had a t�te-�-t�te meeting, a bilateral
meeting. Immediately, the first thing he said

00:09:00.030 --> 00:09:02.970
to me is, what do you intend to do with the
existing problem?

00:09:02.970 --> 00:09:07.890
Now, let me remind you, ladies and gentlemen,
the existing problem was a fiscal consolidation

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:18.390
problem that was attached to the loans, which
reduced nominal GDP by 28 percent, increased

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:24.110
unemployment from 8 (percent) to 28 percent,
ensured that there was no bank credit even

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:28.310
to profitable firms, and effectively crashed
the economy.

00:09:28.310 --> 00:09:32.861
So I said to him, look, Jeroen, this is a
problem that clearly has not worked. And the

00:09:32.861 --> 00:09:38.590
reason why I'm here, why I was elected, was
because the Greek people have had enough with

00:09:38.590 --> 00:09:41.660
it.
Now, at the same time, I understand that the

00:09:41.660 --> 00:09:46.530
states have continued it, which means that
we have a commitment to the program because

00:09:46.530 --> 00:09:52.470
previous governments have signed it. But,
look, in democracies, like Europe is a democracy,

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:57.510
when there are two clashing principles-on
the one hand the principle of continuity and

00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:02.680
on the other the principle of rationality
and democracy-what do you do? You find common

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:07.550
ground. And he turned around and said, well,
that won't work. If you insist on renegotiating

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:12.070
this package, your banks will be closed on
the 28th of February. That was on the 27th

00:10:12.070 --> 00:10:16.770
of January.
So this was a clear clash that was engineered

00:10:16.770 --> 00:10:25.230
on the other side. And the only way of averting
it-and this was our outside option if you

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:32.190
want in game theoretical terms, or our leverage-we
had a bunch of bonds, Greek government bonds,

00:10:32.190 --> 00:10:39.020
old Greek government bonds, that the ECB had
purchased under Jean-Claude Trichet in 2011.

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:43.871
And these-this was the last slice of that
Greek debt, which was in Greek law. So it

00:10:43.871 --> 00:10:49.650
was not in U.S. law, New York law. It was
not in London law. It was in Greek law. So

00:10:49.650 --> 00:10:54.520
all I had to do was to sign-I didn't even
have to take it to parliament.

00:10:54.520 --> 00:11:01.740
I had the document ready, a ministerial decree
to postpone payment of these bonds by 20 years.

00:11:01.740 --> 00:11:09.490
And that would be hugely detrimental to Mario
Draghi's quantitative easing, because he had

00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:14.130
a major clash with Jens Weidmann, the president
of the Bundesbank, who was taking him to the

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:20.960
Constitutional Court of Germany for violating
the charter of ECB by doing QE. And the only

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:27.900
way that QE was allowed to continue was the
pledge by the president of the ECB that there

00:11:27.900 --> 00:11:33.740
would be no haircuts at all, no restructuring
of any paper at all that the ECB ever buys,

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:39.170
as if this is a credible promise, but nevertheless-especially
the euro. (Laughs.)

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:46.680
And if I restructure that paper, QE would
be in trouble. So as long as we signaled to

00:11:46.680 --> 00:11:51.090
the other side that if you close down our
banks-a unilateral threat-but if you close

00:11:51.090 --> 00:11:57.780
down our banks, then we will restructure that
debt. This would be, in my estimation, sufficient

00:11:57.780 --> 00:12:04.940
to bring the two parties together to do what?
To find the compromise. And unfortunately,

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:10.690
and the reason why it was lost in March/April,
members of my government signals to Mario

00:12:10.690 --> 00:12:14.730
Draghi, don't worry about Varoufakis, we won't
let him do it. So it was all finished by then.

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:18.120
SHEARD: So that was your leverage, that was
your-you mentioned game theory. You're well-known

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:21.530
as an expert on game theory. I think you've
written books, et cetera. And many people,

00:12:21.530 --> 00:12:26.320
I think, in the markets who knew this were
looking at you and saying, well, the minister

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:30.650
must be using game theory here. That was your
leverage. You didn't mention another form

00:12:30.650 --> 00:12:35.150
of leverage that many people thought you had,
which was the ultimate, if you like, threat,

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:40.480
whether it was credible or not, of exiting
the eurozone. But it seemed that what happened

00:12:40.480 --> 00:12:45.460
there was you didn't have a lot of leverage
there because some people in the euro group

00:12:45.460 --> 00:12:51.590
were actually perhaps saying maybe Greece
should leave the eurozone. So did you get

00:12:51.590 --> 00:12:54.340
out-bluffed? Or was that never something that
you-

00:12:54.340 --> 00:13:00.000
VAROUFAKIS: No, it was never a strategy of
mine. And I'll tell you why. But let me touch

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:05.100
upon the game theory. I spent 20, 25 years
teaching game theory and researching game

00:13:05.100 --> 00:13:10.250
theory. And anyone who has ever been taught
by me will confirm that my lesson-my lesson-my

00:13:10.250 --> 00:13:20.780
message to my students was: Game theory is
a wonderful edifice, aesthetic, mathematical

00:13:20.780 --> 00:13:27.310
model by which to train your mind. But do
not confuse that which is interesting with

00:13:27.310 --> 00:13:33.550
that which is useful. And if you try to apply
this in negotiations-in real life negotiations,

00:13:33.550 --> 00:13:38.660
either in the corporate world or in politics-then
you are a danger to others and to yourself.

00:13:38.660 --> 00:13:43.690
(Laughter.)
So I'm clean of this. And actually, I wrote

00:13:43.690 --> 00:13:49.529
an op-ed in The New York Times immediately
after I became finance minister with a title-the

00:13:49.529 --> 00:13:56.510
telling title-"No Time for Games." Greece
was a bankrupt state, still is. We did not

00:13:56.510 --> 00:14:08.279
have either the moral or the analytical reason
to play games and call bluffs. My view was

00:14:08.279 --> 00:14:14.230
very simple. When you're bankrupt, you do
not take another loan until you restructure

00:14:14.230 --> 00:14:19.029
your loans and your operation. This is what
the bankruptcy law in Wall Street will tell

00:14:19.029 --> 00:14:26.050
you. This is what any proper bank will tell
you. It's not left, right, it's just common

00:14:26.050 --> 00:14:29.050
sense.
And this is why I find myself in-you know,

00:14:29.050 --> 00:14:35.680
it took a very deep crisis in Greece to create
circumstances where someone like me, I'm a

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:40.920
left-winger, can say the same thing to a left-wing
audience and to an audience of bankers and

00:14:40.920 --> 00:14:46.610
be met with agreement. So there was no bluffing.
Now, given that there was not going to be

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:55.790
any bluffing. I would never use Grexit as
a tool, as a method for gaining leverage.

00:14:55.790 --> 00:15:00.710
Why? Because I didn't believe in Grexit. I
don't believe in the euro. I think we should

00:15:00.710 --> 00:15:07.150
not have created it. I campaigned against
it. But I also implore people, especially

00:15:07.150 --> 00:15:12.990
friends of mine and ideological friends of
mine, to make the sharp distinction between

00:15:12.990 --> 00:15:17.920
static and dynamic analysis.
It's one thing to say we should not have gotten

00:15:17.920 --> 00:15:23.800
in. It's a very different thing to say we
should now get out. Because once you're in,

00:15:23.800 --> 00:15:28.980
the path that led you in does not exist for
you to backtrack, to reverse upon. You fall

00:15:28.980 --> 00:15:31.500
off a cliff, to put it very bluntly. Go back
to-

00:15:31.500 --> 00:15:33.900
SHEARD: You're at a different part of the
game tree now.

00:15:33.900 --> 00:15:34.900
VAROUFAKIS: Exactly.
SHEARD: You can't go back.

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:37.990
VAROUFAKIS: And you can't go back. That's
it. There's an irreversibility, yeah?

00:15:37.990 --> 00:15:40.690
SHEARD: So let me-I'd like to bring the discussion-
VAROUFAKIS: (Don't be mad at us ?).

00:15:40.690 --> 00:15:46.410
SHEARD: I'd like to bring the discussion to,
you know, the contemporary situation, where

00:15:46.410 --> 00:15:53.120
the negotiations over that program-although
it was announced and the European Stability

00:15:53.120 --> 00:15:58.230
Mechanism signed off on it, et cetera-the
actual first review, and to bring the IMF

00:15:58.230 --> 00:16:03.940
in particular back into the game, is still
going on. And the IMF position seems to be

00:16:03.940 --> 00:16:09.460
a little bit, I guess, similar to yours, that
they take issue with the debt sustainability

00:16:09.460 --> 00:16:15.540
analysis. On the other hand, the euro group
do not want anything that looks like a nominal

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:19.421
haircut. And then you would have people who
claim that, you know, the Greek government

00:16:19.421 --> 00:16:24.900
maybe has been dragging its feet on different
reforms. So there seems to be still a stalemate,

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:30.450
that seems to be getting closer to resolution,
if we take the recent few days. But that's

00:16:30.450 --> 00:16:33.150
my question.
VAROUFAKIS: Have you listened to the discussions

00:16:33.150 --> 00:16:38.610
between Poul Thomsen and Delia Velkouleskou
that was leaked by WikiLeaks? It's worth a

00:16:38.610 --> 00:16:40.010
listen, or a read.
SHEARD: I did. I listened to that.

00:16:40.010 --> 00:16:43.940
VAROUFAKIS: It really is worth it.
SHEARD: I did listen to that. But what is

00:16:43.940 --> 00:16:50.500
your assessment of the situation on the ground
now, the Greek economy, and, you know, is

00:16:50.500 --> 00:16:57.860
this Grexit risk-you may not want Grexit-but
is the Grexit risk going to start to loom

00:16:57.860 --> 00:17:01.589
large again?
VAROUFAKIS: To understand what went wrong

00:17:01.589 --> 00:17:09.069
last year, and the reason why my removal has
not helped, you've got to understand that

00:17:09.069 --> 00:17:18.639
there is a major tussle between three basic
forces-powerful forces. One is Dr. Wolfgang

00:17:18.639 --> 00:17:25.629
Sch�uble. He's a force in himself. Then
there's Merkel, a force in herself. And the

00:17:25.629 --> 00:17:34.419
two forces clash mercilessly over not just
Greece, over the eurozone. They have completely

00:17:34.419 --> 00:17:42.390
different views. And one of the tragedies
of being a pipsqueak little country like Greece,

00:17:42.390 --> 00:17:47.251
whose population is suffering the ill effects
of a great depression, is that you have to

00:17:47.251 --> 00:17:51.629
wait until the elephants stop tussling and
trampling upon you.

00:17:51.629 --> 00:18:01.119
And then, of course, there is the IMF. There
are different agendas here. The IMF is particularly

00:18:01.119 --> 00:18:07.370
incensed with itself for having allowed itself
to get into the Greek problem in 2010. They

00:18:07.370 --> 00:18:14.070
blame Strauss-Kahn. They should blame everyone
in there. But nevertheless, they have violated

00:18:14.070 --> 00:18:20.129
their charter to be there because the IMF
has no right to lend to an insolvent state.

00:18:20.129 --> 00:18:27.510
They lent to an insolvent state in 2010, and
then they lent again in 2012. And last year,

00:18:27.510 --> 00:18:33.100
they consented once more. They didn't lend
in money, but they played along with the extension

00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:39.159
in potential. So they want to find a way out.
The first time I met Poul Thomsen and Christine

00:18:39.159 --> 00:18:45.460
Lagarde, they were more radical than I am.
I was trying-you know, I was struggling to

00:18:45.460 --> 00:18:51.320
find a balance between what was necessary
to do with the Greek debt, and what was politically

00:18:51.320 --> 00:18:59.009
palatable in Berlin in order to find an agreement,
to be pragmatic about it. So knowing that

00:18:59.009 --> 00:19:04.549
a nominal haircut is political poison for
the German leadership I was not proposing

00:19:04.549 --> 00:19:09.440
one. I was proposing very straightforward
debt swaps, things that financial engineering

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:15.789
conjures up within five seconds. So take-we
had the debt of 30 billion, but those bonds

00:19:15.789 --> 00:19:23.000
that I mentioned before with the ECB, spikey,
short-term, nasty. Well, park them on the

00:19:23.000 --> 00:19:28.350
ESM. And now we'll take a 30 billion (dollar)
loan from ESM and give it to the-to the Central

00:19:28.350 --> 00:19:34.289
Bank, receive from the Central Bank the super
profits that they made, because they, of course,

00:19:34.289 --> 00:19:39.059
purchased those bonds far below par, put them
in an escrow account. Don't give them to me.

00:19:39.059 --> 00:19:44.299
I don't trust myself. I'm Greek. (Laughter.)
Put them in an escrow account and have that

00:19:44.299 --> 00:19:48.590
escrow account repay the IMF in the next two
years. It will be enough.

00:19:48.590 --> 00:19:56.940
So that way-and then tie up the repayment
schedule to the ESM to nominal GDP growth

00:19:56.940 --> 00:20:03.220
and with certain minimum levels of nominal
GDP before you trigger the repayments. In

00:20:03.220 --> 00:20:08.780
the end, they took these proposals of mine.
They applied to Ukraine, not to Greece. Somebody

00:20:08.780 --> 00:20:10.430
benefited.
SHEARD: They kind of did half of it with the

00:20:10.430 --> 00:20:13.869
terming out of the debt, though, in this 86
billion (dollar) package.

00:20:13.869 --> 00:20:18.080
VAROUFAKIS: The 86-well, no, what did they
do with the 86 billion (dollars)? They did

00:20:18.080 --> 00:20:21.962
none of this. What they-what they did was
they took another 86 billion (dollars) from

00:20:21.962 --> 00:20:25.639
one pocket of the troika and gave it to another.
But there was no debt restructuring there.

00:20:25.639 --> 00:20:28.200
SHEARD: But there has been terming-lengthening
of maturity, correct?

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:29.420
VAROUFAKIS: No.
SHEARD: No?

00:20:29.420 --> 00:20:35.850
VAROUFAKIS: No, not now. There was in 2012.
But still the-(chuckles)-the debt is absolutely

00:20:35.850 --> 00:20:40.470
unpayable, as the IMF says. So just to give
you, Paul-let me-let me give you an example

00:20:40.470 --> 00:20:45.169
of the situation we found ourselves in. It
was just absolutely maddening.

00:20:45.169 --> 00:20:52.350
Twenty-fifth of June was when-you mentioned
before I was presented to the Eurogroup with

00:20:52.350 --> 00:20:58.230
the ultimatum. It was the first time I received
a single sheet of (aid forwarded ?) from the

00:20:58.230 --> 00:21:02.889
creditors.
During those periods, those five months of

00:21:02.889 --> 00:21:09.269
negotiations, we-my office would give them
constantly debt-swap ideas, ideas about reforms,

00:21:09.269 --> 00:21:14.950
about a new IRS independent of the ministry,
asking for the help of the German Finance

00:21:14.950 --> 00:21:19.559
Ministry. All that-you know, a lot of paper
was going their way. I had received not one

00:21:19.559 --> 00:21:24.299
page of (aid forwarded ?) from them. And the
international especially financial press was

00:21:24.299 --> 00:21:28.419
reporting that we had no proposals and we
were stalling.

00:21:28.419 --> 00:21:33.770
OK, so the first piece of paper arrives 25th
of June, right-five days before the banks

00:21:33.770 --> 00:21:43.059
were meant to close. And I look at it. I was
given 10 minutes prior to the Eurogroup meeting.

00:21:43.059 --> 00:21:48.450
Can you imagine it? It was 120 pages. I went
through it very quickly with my team, two

00:21:48.450 --> 00:21:55.220
or three people, and I walked in there. I
heard the views of the leaders of the institutions,

00:21:55.220 --> 00:21:57.760
and then I started unpacking it and asking
them questions.

00:21:57.760 --> 00:22:02.940
So the first question went to the Central
Bank. I said, I notice here that in your funding

00:22:02.940 --> 00:22:11.799
proposal you are cannibalizing the bank recapitalization
fund to use it to repay the ECB in the short

00:22:11.799 --> 00:22:17.330
term. And where will the recapitalization
funding come from? And then the answer from

00:22:17.330 --> 00:22:23.190
Benoit Coeure and Mario Draghi was what we
do-we will replenish this by means of a new

00:22:23.190 --> 00:22:25.639
loan from the ESM.
And then I look at Wolfgang Sch�uble and

00:22:25.639 --> 00:22:30.720
I said, Wolfgang, do you accept that? They
are embedding a third program into the second

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:35.779
program. To make the second program-to extend
the second program, they are creating a new

00:22:35.779 --> 00:22:39.090
program, a new loan, and they're embedding
it in the second one. Can you pass this through

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:42.690
the Bundestag? He said, no, I cannot pass
it through the Bundestag. There is no-and

00:22:42.690 --> 00:22:47.549
he started shouting at Draghi. The two of
them nearly had a fistfight, seriously, in

00:22:47.549 --> 00:22:51.860
front of me. Then I asked-
SHEARD: I think you're making some breaking

00:22:51.860 --> 00:22:56.059
news here, Yanis. (Laughter.)
VAROUFAKIS: Well, you know, I'm not an insider.

00:22:56.059 --> 00:23:00.409
(Laughter.) And that's why I-as I said at
the beginning, this is why I appreciate having

00:23:00.409 --> 00:23:06.799
this exchange for-you know, in order to go
beyond appearances and beyond what was reported.

00:23:06.799 --> 00:23:15.730
And, OK, so then I asked Christine. I said,
I see here there is a DSA analysis, a debt

00:23:15.730 --> 00:23:21.830
sustainability analysis. Does this have the
approval of the IMF? Because the way I look

00:23:21.830 --> 00:23:26.799
at it, the figures don't add up, and I can't
believe that your staff in Washington, D.C.

00:23:26.799 --> 00:23:33.390
have approved it. She concurred: they haven't,
and that the IMF is not backing those numbers.

00:23:33.390 --> 00:23:38.340
But then I said to her, so is this also an
ultimatum from you? Is this an ultimatum from

00:23:38.340 --> 00:23:43.019
the troika or only from the Commission? Does
the IMF say to me that this is take it or

00:23:43.019 --> 00:23:48.820
leave it? And she had to say, yes, but we
have to look again at the numbers. So what

00:23:48.820 --> 00:23:54.429
kind of an ultimatum is this? (Laughter.)
So they started fighting amongst themselves.

00:23:54.429 --> 00:23:59.750
And Dijsselbloem, the president of the Eurogroup,
interrupts it and says to me, again, you have

00:23:59.750 --> 00:24:04.899
to tell me now whether you're accepting it
or not. And if you insist on renegotiating

00:24:04.899 --> 00:24:08.830
the numbers, it will be casus belli-casus
belli.

00:24:08.830 --> 00:24:16.330
And I said, this is not a language that I
understand, Mr. President. And in any case,

00:24:16.330 --> 00:24:21.840
I would like to ask the Secretariat whether
the president of the Eurogroup has the authority

00:24:21.840 --> 00:24:28.529
to threaten the minister of finance with casus
belli. There was a kerfuffle in the Secretariat.

00:24:28.529 --> 00:24:34.609
(Laughter.) There was an adjournment of 10,
20 minutes. And then-there were phone calls

00:24:34.609 --> 00:24:42.230
being made. I was just watching that in a
combination of amusement and horror.

00:24:42.230 --> 00:24:49.121
And then the Secretariat-somebody from the
Secretariat said, Mr. Minister, we have to

00:24:49.121 --> 00:24:57.399
inform you that the Eurogroup is an informal
meeting of finance ministers. It is not constituted

00:24:57.399 --> 00:25:03.029
European law or treaty, and therefore it does
not exist legally. So the president of the

00:25:03.029 --> 00:25:07.340
Eurogroup is at will to do what he wants.
(Laughter.) I mean, this is the situation

00:25:07.340 --> 00:25:09.559
we were facing.
SHEARD: And that's basically when the referendum

00:25:09.559 --> 00:25:12.300
got called.
VAROUFAKIS: And at that point the prime minister

00:25:12.300 --> 00:25:19.010
said, OK. And I think he had a point there,
even though we disagreed on-by that time we

00:25:19.010 --> 00:25:27.989
had very serious disagreements, but he had
a point. Here is a piece of paper that Europe-organized,

00:25:27.989 --> 00:25:32.919
civilized Europe-is giving a small bankrupt
nation and saying take it or leave it.

00:25:32.919 --> 00:25:38.389
Now, I was not elected to have a clash with
Europe. At the same time, I was not elected

00:25:38.389 --> 00:25:45.149
to accept a piece of paper that was not worth
the paper it was written on from a funding-financial-fiscal-reform

00:25:45.149 --> 00:25:50.200
point of view. So what do you do at that point,
as a responsible government? You take it to

00:25:50.200 --> 00:25:55.309
the Greek people and you say, well, here is
this. Study it. We will give you recommendations.

00:25:55.309 --> 00:26:01.129
The opposition parties can give you recommendations.
The troika can come to Greece and campaign

00:26:01.129 --> 00:26:04.679
in favor of that piece of paper. And you decide.
(Chuckles.)

00:26:04.679 --> 00:26:12.999
At which point I had finance ministers telling
me, are you serious? You're going to ask voters

00:26:12.999 --> 00:26:18.131
to make complicated decisions about their
future? I said, yes, it's democracy. (Laughter.)

00:26:18.131 --> 00:26:21.330
It's a terrible system, as Winston Churchill
said, but it's the best of all alternatives,

00:26:21.330 --> 00:26:26.840
isn't it? Do you know any better system? So,
you know, that's how it worked.

00:26:26.840 --> 00:26:30.669
SHEARD: Greece has been in the news, obviously,
in the last-well, more than a few months for

00:26:30.669 --> 00:26:34.529
another issue, of course, which is that it
is at the front line of the refugee crisis

00:26:34.529 --> 00:26:41.859
in Europe. What effect is this having on the
Greek economy, the administrative capacity?

00:26:41.859 --> 00:26:49.489
You know, that has also, you know, always
been a question mark in Greece. And how does

00:26:49.489 --> 00:26:55.240
this refugee crisis kind of change the calculus,
if at all, of all of this stuff that we're

00:26:55.240 --> 00:26:59.009
talking about, which is still ongoing, trying
to reach agreement about, you know, what to

00:26:59.009 --> 00:27:05.409
do with the Greek rescue package, et cetera?
VAROUFAKIS: In a humane, well-functioning

00:27:05.409 --> 00:27:14.529
Europe, the two issues should be utterly independent.
One has to do with our own terrible monetary

00:27:14.529 --> 00:27:21.450
architecture. This is a family feud, a mess.
We should just find a way of correcting it,

00:27:21.450 --> 00:27:25.309
of fixing it.
The second issue is a humanitarian issue.

00:27:25.309 --> 00:27:30.960
There are people who are knocking on your
door in the middle of the night, drowning,

00:27:30.960 --> 00:27:38.210
wet. They've been shot at. They've been chased
out of their homes by various vulgar armies.

00:27:38.210 --> 00:27:46.320
At that moment, what do you do?
I think the best strategy and the best policy

00:27:46.320 --> 00:27:53.510
was articulated by a 90-year-old woman on
Greek television a couple of weeks ago. She's

00:27:53.510 --> 00:27:59.169
a poor woman, somewhere in-(inaudible)-the
Greek-Macedonian border. She has a farmhouse.

00:27:59.169 --> 00:28:06.090
She has a few sheep. She's poor. And there
were about seven or eight migrants, refugees,

00:28:06.090 --> 00:28:09.529
that were loitering around her place, and
she was feeding them. And this television

00:28:09.529 --> 00:28:15.919
crew invades her property and shove a microphone
in her face and say, why did you let them

00:28:15.919 --> 00:28:22.970
in? And she said, oh, they were hungry.
To me, that is the solution. You know, when

00:28:22.970 --> 00:28:26.309
somebody knocks on your door in the middle
of the night, you do not do a cost-benefit

00:28:26.309 --> 00:28:32.971
analysis and you don't mix this problem with
whatever problems your household has. Europe

00:28:32.971 --> 00:28:35.740
has many problems, but this should be kept
completely separate.

00:28:35.740 --> 00:28:41.789
Let me say that, on the question of what kind
of an impact it had on Greece, by taking you

00:28:41.789 --> 00:28:47.789
back a few years. You know, when the Iron
Curtain collapsed in 1991, does-is anyone

00:28:47.789 --> 00:28:55.450
familiar with the fact that 1 million refugees
from the other side of the Iron Curtain came

00:28:55.450 --> 00:29:00.610
into Greece? One million. We have a small
country of 10 million, so 10 percent of an

00:29:00.610 --> 00:29:06.159
influx within a few months. Half of Albania
came to Greece. Just literally, half of the

00:29:06.159 --> 00:29:12.229
population of Albania came to Greece.
Do you know what happened? Nothing. They're

00:29:12.229 --> 00:29:17.529
still there. Some of them-their kids are some
of my best students at the University of Athens.

00:29:17.529 --> 00:29:21.280
They have an impeccable Greek accent. I can't
tell the difference between their accent and

00:29:21.280 --> 00:29:25.960
the accent of Greeks. I only notice their
surname being different. Greece is a much

00:29:25.960 --> 00:29:32.729
stronger, much richer country as a result
of this influx of migrants.

00:29:32.729 --> 00:29:41.119
Europe is rich enough, powerful enough, big
enough to be able to deal with this without

00:29:41.119 --> 00:29:46.120
anyone noticing the problem. You can take
half of them, take them-put them outside of

00:29:46.120 --> 00:29:54.879
Glasgow. It's empty. The food would get better
in Scotland. (Laughter.) The reason why we

00:29:54.879 --> 00:30:01.970
have this xenophobic, moral panic about refugees
is because the economic foundations of Europe

00:30:01.970 --> 00:30:08.559
are very brittle and they are becoming more
brittle by the day due to the inane handling

00:30:08.559 --> 00:30:14.850
of an inevitable crisis of a currency which
was not meant to survive the trials and tribulations

00:30:14.850 --> 00:30:18.850
and shockwaves of global capitalism.
SHEARD: Well, I think that might be an opportune

00:30:18.850 --> 00:30:24.269
point to actually go to our members. So I'd
like to invite the members here to join the

00:30:24.269 --> 00:30:29.549
conversation. Remind you this is on the record.
Could you indicate your interest in speaking,

00:30:29.549 --> 00:30:33.249
wait for the microphone to come, stand up
if you wouldn't mind-so the gentleman in the

00:30:33.249 --> 00:30:39.320
back in there-state your name and affiliation
and just ask one very concise question please.

00:30:39.320 --> 00:30:45.789
Q: I will. Minister Varoufakis, very, very
interesting. I'm Ronald Tiersky from Amherst

00:30:45.789 --> 00:30:50.489
College. I'm not a banker. I'm not a hedge
fund person.

00:30:50.489 --> 00:30:59.159
Looking back on it now, do you think it was
a mistake for you to go into the government,

00:30:59.159 --> 00:31:06.259
with all that happened, or do you think it
was exactly the right thing to do?

00:31:06.259 --> 00:31:15.470
VAROUFAKIS: It is true that if I knew how
we would be divided, and that the original

00:31:15.470 --> 00:31:21.359
pact we had was brittler than I thought, it
might have thought twice about it. But having

00:31:21.359 --> 00:31:26.789
said that, with the benefit of hindsight,
I'm pleased that I went in, because-not just

00:31:26.789 --> 00:31:35.170
for Greeks. Europeans today understand a lot
better the problems of governance of the European

00:31:35.170 --> 00:31:41.769
Union as a result of having brought out to
the open all these shenanigans that are taking

00:31:41.769 --> 00:31:49.279
place within. When I go to Germany, when I
go to France, when I go to Spain, I discuss,

00:31:49.279 --> 00:31:54.029
come across people, citizens, very concerned
Europeans who can see the disintegration of

00:31:54.029 --> 00:32:01.390
Europe and all the threats that this is bringing
to their doorstep.

00:32:01.390 --> 00:32:07.009
And they are appreciative of the fact that
now they understand the profound problems

00:32:07.009 --> 00:32:14.519
of governance. We've started something which
will certainly prove beneficial in the sense

00:32:14.519 --> 00:32:20.909
that an understanding, a grasp of what goes
on behind closed doors-especially when what

00:32:20.909 --> 00:32:27.710
goes on behind closed doors is simply a recapitulation
of failed policies for reasons that have absolutely

00:32:27.710 --> 00:32:32.730
nothing to do with economic rationality, humanism,
or democracy. This is an important contribution.

00:32:32.730 --> 00:32:38.250
And I feel that I contributed something to
it, even though we lost the battle.

00:32:38.250 --> 00:32:49.659
SHEARD: To the lady down here.
Q: Thanks. I'm Lucy Komisar. I'm a journalist.

00:32:49.659 --> 00:33:00.059
The groups, individuals, EU representatives
attacking Greece said in one area that Greece

00:33:00.059 --> 00:33:10.940
was not collecting the proper taxes that were
due it from individuals, from business corporations.

00:33:10.940 --> 00:33:20.659
Did you raise the question with them that
countries such as France and the U.K., Luxembourg

00:33:20.659 --> 00:33:28.869
run tax havens where these businesses and
individuals stash their money so that they

00:33:28.869 --> 00:33:33.249
don't pay taxes?
VAROUFAKIS: Well, I didn't have to because,

00:33:33.249 --> 00:33:37.609
you know, in Europe-the president of the European
Commission was running the largest tax haven

00:33:37.609 --> 00:33:43.539
in the world. It's called Luxembourg, yeah?
So when he wants-he said we can talk to him.

00:33:43.539 --> 00:33:52.619
He looked at me, and he gave me a little lecture
on tax collection. I said, really? (Laughter.)

00:33:52.619 --> 00:33:58.989
But, no, let me make this point. There is
no doubt that we had a serious problem of

00:33:58.989 --> 00:34:07.889
tax evasion and, even worse, tax immunity
in Greece. Those in power managing to manipulate

00:34:07.889 --> 00:34:11.810
the system in such a way that they didn't
have to pay tax. They didn't have to evade

00:34:11.810 --> 00:34:17.250
taxes because they didn't have to pay them.
That is a chronic problem in Greece.

00:34:17.250 --> 00:34:21.890
I have to say that this has improved a great
deal for-so for sad reasons. The powers that

00:34:21.890 --> 00:34:28.600
be have already taken their money out long
ago from Greece. And those who are left behind

00:34:28.600 --> 00:34:35.040
don't have a way of tax evading anymore. So
the degree of tax evasion has been severely

00:34:35.040 --> 00:34:42.920
circumscribed of recent, but that's not for
good reasons. But let me relate to you the

00:34:42.920 --> 00:34:50.300
most traumatic experience I've had, more traumatic
than negotiations or anything of the sort.

00:34:50.300 --> 00:34:57.920
I saw my job for the five months I was in
government as effectively having two pillars.

00:34:57.920 --> 00:35:01.010
One was the negotiations and the other was
tax evasion.

00:35:01.010 --> 00:35:12.210
So from day one, I put together, I convened
a working group for homing in on the major

00:35:12.210 --> 00:35:20.970
tax evaders, yeah? So I put together two teams
within the ministry, one from the equivalent

00:35:20.970 --> 00:35:28.090
of the IRS and the other one from IT, because
you've got to realize that through austerity

00:35:28.090 --> 00:35:33.390
cuts and so on we didn't even have the personnel
to go after the tax evaders in the way that

00:35:33.390 --> 00:35:37.290
it would happen here in the United States.
So the way we did it was I leaned very, very

00:35:37.290 --> 00:35:45.860
heavily upon the banks to get real-time data
of all transactions within the country and

00:35:45.860 --> 00:35:51.960
without the country for the last 15, 20 years.
That took two, three months, and a lot of

00:35:51.960 --> 00:35:58.010
leaning on bankers to do it, but we got it.
And then it was-I thought it was a simple

00:35:58.010 --> 00:36:04.480
matter of creating an algorithmic system,
software, that would compare all the transactions

00:36:04.480 --> 00:36:10.601
from tax file number to tax file number with
tax returns. I had a lot of resistance form

00:36:10.601 --> 00:36:15.630
the Greek IRS, which I didn't control-not
because it's independent, but because it was

00:36:15.630 --> 00:36:21.981
answerable directly to the troika, our lenders.
And in any case, I banged a few heads, put

00:36:21.981 --> 00:36:31.320
together this working group. Preliminary results
in late May of 2015 revealed more than 480,000

00:36:31.320 --> 00:36:37.410
tax file numbers that had evaded more than
$150,000. That is a lot of money for the Greek-for

00:36:37.410 --> 00:36:42.400
the small, Greek state. And some of them it
was millions, yeah?

00:36:42.400 --> 00:36:50.110
And this group was going to come to its final
findings in October of last year. And our

00:36:50.110 --> 00:36:56.160
idea was very simple. Once we had the final
list, instead of going through the process

00:36:56.160 --> 00:37:00.160
of prosecuting and all that, we would just
announce that this is what we've done, without

00:37:00.160 --> 00:37:04.950
any names at all, and we would say, OK, now
here is a website. You can go there, and you

00:37:04.950 --> 00:37:11.710
have a week to pay what you think you owe.
And if you do, we will give you tax immunity-immunity

00:37:11.710 --> 00:37:18.180
from prosecution-and we would turn a blind
eye. That was the plan. Now, let me tell you

00:37:18.180 --> 00:37:23.980
that the greatest resistance to this came
firstly from the oligarchs within Greece who

00:37:23.980 --> 00:37:28.720
were connected to the Greek IRS, with great
cover from the troika.

00:37:28.720 --> 00:37:34.330
And this is-so this sounds a bit strange.
But remember, during those months I was in

00:37:34.330 --> 00:37:42.160
office the press-the Greek press, television,
radio, newspapers, that is owned by the oligarchs,

00:37:42.160 --> 00:37:48.100
was completely and utterly in support of the
troika against me and against the prime minister.

00:37:48.100 --> 00:37:55.100
And they were getting some cover for it. OK,
6th of July I resign, 8th of July, the head

00:37:55.100 --> 00:38:02.100
of this working group calls me-I was no longer
the minister, but I had appointed him-in tears,

00:38:02.100 --> 00:38:09.220
saying that the troika is trying to disband
the unit. Thirteenth of August, the memorandum

00:38:09.220 --> 00:38:17.570
was signed, my prime minister had capitulated.
In it, it stated clearly that this working

00:38:17.570 --> 00:38:23.920
group is disbanded and absorbed by the original
IRS, which was doing nothing. So the troika

00:38:23.920 --> 00:38:29.180
is the oligarchs and the tax evaders' best
friend. It's very difficult to explain this

00:38:29.180 --> 00:38:33.810
to people.
SHEARD: Yes, OK. The lady at the table over

00:38:33.810 --> 00:38:39.050
here. Thank you.
Q: Hello. Kay Boulware, John Jay College of

00:38:39.050 --> 00:38:42.040
Criminal Justice. Thank you, sir, for your
remarks.

00:38:42.040 --> 00:38:48.750
I would like-could you comment on the land
registry system in Greece, as conductive or

00:38:48.750 --> 00:38:51.340
not conductive for investment, given that
land-

00:38:51.340 --> 00:38:56.930
VAROUFAKIS: Well, it will be conductive when
it's finished. We started about 90 years ago-(laughter)-and

00:38:56.930 --> 00:39:01.920
we're still trying to complete it. This is
a sad story.

00:39:01.920 --> 00:39:05.460
SHEARD: And what is the issue there with the
land registry? What is the problem you're

00:39:05.460 --> 00:39:09.400
trying to solve?
VAROUFAKIS: Well, the problem is that we still-I'll

00:39:09.400 --> 00:39:17.000
tell you a personal experience. When my mother
died, she left me a plot of land. And then

00:39:17.000 --> 00:39:23.710
when I tried-you know, when my lawyer tried
to look into the deeds and all that, it was

00:39:23.710 --> 00:39:29.710
discovered that it was impossible to pin down
exactly what I owe-I own. And it goes back

00:39:29.710 --> 00:39:37.970
to the Ottoman Empire, the various disputes
on, you know, delineating the plot of land,

00:39:37.970 --> 00:39:47.300
which is not worth anything anyway.
So this is very old land with very long memories

00:39:47.300 --> 00:39:55.810
of territorial disputes. And it's not a technical
issue. The technical tools are there for completing

00:39:55.810 --> 00:40:02.580
the land registry, but then the technicians
who are responsible for computerizing and

00:40:02.580 --> 00:40:10.970
digitizing the land registry hit on the wall
of these long-lasting legal battles between

00:40:10.970 --> 00:40:18.480
clans and various different jurisdictions,
because Greece was not liberated all in one

00:40:18.480 --> 00:40:22.940
go from the Ottoman Empire. Anyway, it's a
mess. I'm not a lawyer. I don't understand

00:40:22.940 --> 00:40:27.230
it, but it is taking ages.
From an economic point of view, the problem

00:40:27.230 --> 00:40:31.900
with this is that it is very difficult to
establish property rights and therefore to

00:40:31.900 --> 00:40:37.420
create markets that are efficient. This is
the long and the short of it, but we're getting

00:40:37.420 --> 00:40:46.550
closer on this issue.
SHEARD: David. Sorry, over here, David Malpass.

00:40:46.550 --> 00:40:53.030
Q: Hi. David Malpass with Encima Global.
Could you comment some on the pensions of

00:40:53.030 --> 00:40:59.810
public sector workers, including the military?
And it's not-it's not just a problem of Greece

00:40:59.810 --> 00:41:06.890
or an issue-it applies to Puerto Rico, here,
to Illinois, even to New York State. How do

00:41:06.890 --> 00:41:15.340
you-how do you compare those needs, the needs
of those pension commitments, to the creditors'

00:41:15.340 --> 00:41:19.460
needs within that kind of a debt restructuring?
Thanks.

00:41:19.460 --> 00:41:25.750
VAROUFAKIS: Excellent question. Thank you,
sir. We have so many different problems with

00:41:25.750 --> 00:41:30.760
our pension system. Let me mention two or
three, just in brief.

00:41:30.760 --> 00:41:37.800
The first one is we don't have a problem-a
proper social security system in Greece, which

00:41:37.800 --> 00:41:46.640
unloads a lot of burdens onto the pension
system. So in properly constructed democracies,

00:41:46.640 --> 00:41:53.800
liberal democracies, the separation of social
security from pensions allows pensions-pension

00:41:53.800 --> 00:42:01.130
funds to be viable and solvent.
So in Germany, here in the United States,

00:42:01.130 --> 00:42:07.330
if you are a 62-year-old unemployed worker
in a Rust Belt, and it's very difficult for

00:42:07.330 --> 00:42:12.750
you to retrain to find a job in the area or
to be retrained and to be sent to another

00:42:12.750 --> 00:42:18.340
state-you're coming close to retirement age
anyway-you go on Social Security, and then

00:42:18.340 --> 00:42:24.080
at some point, when you're 65, 66, 67, you
get transferred to the pension fund. In places

00:42:24.080 --> 00:42:28.960
like Greece-and I suppose the same thing applies
to Puerto Rico and to various other states,

00:42:28.960 --> 00:42:35.860
less-developed states-there is no Social Security.
So the 62-year-old will either starve or there

00:42:35.860 --> 00:42:40.780
will be political pressure to put him or her
onto the pension system. And then the pension

00:42:40.780 --> 00:42:45.340
fund becomes decreasingly solvent. So that's
one example.

00:42:45.340 --> 00:42:51.720
The second problem we had in Greece was in
2012 there was a haircut of Greek debt, but

00:42:51.720 --> 00:42:59.300
it was partial and it hit pension funds. So
it was called PSI, the private sector involvement,

00:42:59.300 --> 00:43:05.570
something like that, some euphemism-Europe
is fantastic at euphemisms. It was a haircut

00:43:05.570 --> 00:43:11.700
of private sector debt that was not covered
by the powers that be. So the banks lost a

00:43:11.700 --> 00:43:16.650
lot of money out of the government bonds that
were haircut, but immediately they got a capital

00:43:16.650 --> 00:43:23.730
infusion from the bailouts. So they were made
whole in the end. The pension funds were not.

00:43:23.730 --> 00:43:29.730
The pension funds lost 20 billion (dollars).
That's a lot of money for a small country's

00:43:29.730 --> 00:43:35.520
pension funds. So immediately their capitalization
was destroyed.

00:43:35.520 --> 00:43:41.770
And then the troika comes in with the-(inaudible)-and
they say, OK, so you need-(inaudible)-in order

00:43:41.770 --> 00:43:49.850
to balance the books of the pension funds.
Yes, but, you know, set aside the social justice

00:43:49.850 --> 00:43:56.930
issue; just forget about it. Just look at
the economics of it. If you look at the microeconomic

00:43:56.930 --> 00:44:02.540
impacts, it adds to the debt deflationary
cycle, because-I'm going to give you a very

00:44:02.540 --> 00:44:10.710
startle statistic: 1 in 2 families have no
working member in Greece, as we speak. It's

00:44:10.710 --> 00:44:16.110
that bad, 1 in 2, and they survive on the
basis of some small pension.

00:44:16.110 --> 00:44:22.780
If you reduce that pension in order to balance
the books of the pension funds, you may balance

00:44:22.780 --> 00:44:26.900
the books of the pension funds in the very,
very short run, but the multiplier effect,

00:44:26.900 --> 00:44:32.050
the negative multiplier effect of this pension
reduction is massive. Small businesses will

00:44:32.050 --> 00:44:38.890
close in areas where it's the pensions that
keep them alive. And these people will stop

00:44:38.890 --> 00:44:44.770
contributing to their pension funds and the
downward spiral proceeds.

00:44:44.770 --> 00:44:50.870
So this was what I was struggling to impress
upon the good people of the IMF, the CB, and

00:44:50.870 --> 00:44:55.010
the commission, that they must not think in
terms of pure accounting; they must think

00:44:55.010 --> 00:45:01.060
of the negative multiplier accelerator effects
of what they do in order to balance books,

00:45:01.060 --> 00:45:06.070
which in the end never get balanced when you
ignore the dynamic microeconomic effects of

00:45:06.070 --> 00:45:09.250
what you're doing.
SHEARD: Sounds very Keynesian. (Laughter.)

00:45:09.250 --> 00:45:12.720
VAROUFAKIS: Well, it's common sense. I mean,
even Friedman would agree.

00:45:12.720 --> 00:45:15.170
SHEARD: Well, Keynes was a great economist.
VAROUFAKIS: I think that Milton Friedman would

00:45:15.170 --> 00:45:16.290
agree with it.
SHEARD: That was not a-

00:45:16.290 --> 00:45:18.920
VAROUFAKIS: I don't think that Milton would
disagree with that.

00:45:18.920 --> 00:45:21.410
SHEARD: I think a lot of economists would
agree with that.

00:45:21.410 --> 00:45:28.230
I'll come to you, Ben, but down that table
there. Yes.

00:45:28.230 --> 00:45:32.810
Q: Thank you very much. Shane Velli (ph) from
Soros.

00:45:32.810 --> 00:45:35.310
You talked about-
VAROUFAKIS: I know who you are. (Laughter.)

00:45:35.310 --> 00:45:37.540
Q: We have a history.
VAROUFAKIS: We have a long history.

00:45:37.540 --> 00:45:44.190
Q: You talked about the past. If you could
talk a bit about the future, because Europeans

00:45:44.190 --> 00:45:50.310
seem to be stuck between a rock and a hard
place and with sort of conflicting feelings.

00:45:50.310 --> 00:45:57.290
On one hand, they're terrified about leaving
the euro-and you seem to be part of that-in

00:45:57.290 --> 00:46:01.040
part because, I agree, I think Europeans believe
in what you've described and sort of the dynamic

00:46:01.040 --> 00:46:07.610
of that. And at the same time, Europeans are
increasingly disenchanted and worried about

00:46:07.610 --> 00:46:12.040
the EU, European institutions, and the lack
of democracy.

00:46:12.040 --> 00:46:18.000
So you seem to need a new settlement to resolve
these two views, and this new settlement seems

00:46:18.000 --> 00:46:23.870
to be very far away, in part because France
and Germany, the two countries that should

00:46:23.870 --> 00:46:28.942
be instrumental in bringing this new settlement
together, seem to be so far apart. So I know

00:46:28.942 --> 00:46:33.880
you've started a new movement that tries to
bring about this new settlement. Could you

00:46:33.880 --> 00:46:37.720
describe about how it would work? And could
you tell us if you have hope that this new

00:46:37.720 --> 00:46:42.340
settlement can come before the next 10 years,
because it looks that we're trapped into a

00:46:42.340 --> 00:46:47.710
muddle-through scenario that could last forever.
SHEARD: And if I could sort of add to that

00:46:47.710 --> 00:46:52.280
question, you know, the problems of the architecture
in the European Union and the eurozone in

00:46:52.280 --> 00:46:58.080
particular of course are well-recognized even
by the elites in Brussels and elsewhere, with

00:46:58.080 --> 00:47:00.160
the Four Presidents' Report back in 2012-
VAROUFAKIS: Five.

00:47:00.160 --> 00:47:03.170
SHEARD: -and then the Five Presidents' Report-
VAROUFAKIS: Oh, yes.

00:47:03.170 --> 00:47:08.170
SHEARD: -in 2015, laying out a kind of long-term
roadmap. So what do you make of that? And

00:47:08.170 --> 00:47:12.780
what would your vision be for ultimately what
Europe, in some sense, would need to look

00:47:12.780 --> 00:47:18.120
like architecture-wise for it really to work
and fulfill its promise?

00:47:18.120 --> 00:47:24.000
VAROUFAKIS: In Europe we have two problems,
and the two questions I just received highlight

00:47:24.000 --> 00:47:28.320
them.
One is that we have created the gold standard

00:47:28.320 --> 00:47:33.390
in the middle of Europe. As we know, the gold
standard is pretty good during the good times

00:47:33.390 --> 00:47:38.540
and it's really appalling in bad times. We
have a currency which is schizophrenic. On

00:47:38.540 --> 00:47:48.910
the one hand, it is-it resembles a fixed exchange
rate regime where the only serious mechanism

00:47:48.910 --> 00:47:54.310
for adjustment during a crisis is exit, but
then exit is catastrophic because you don't

00:47:54.310 --> 00:48:00.020
have a peg to sever like the peso-dollar peg,
which was just severed overnight. Here you

00:48:00.020 --> 00:48:03.470
don't have a currency that has a peg with
the euro to sever.

00:48:03.470 --> 00:48:08.670
You have to create one, and it takes 10-12
months to create one. So it's like devaluing

00:48:08.670 --> 00:48:16.470
but announcing you will do so 10-12 months
in advance, so thus the panic at the idea

00:48:16.470 --> 00:48:23.020
of getting out. So it's partly fix the exchange
rate regime, and then it's-but also it's the

00:48:23.020 --> 00:48:28.030
same currency without really being state money
because there is no state behind it, no federal

00:48:28.030 --> 00:48:34.090
state. So this is one problem.
The second problem is that, unlike the American

00:48:34.090 --> 00:48:37.491
government, unlike the British government,
and unlike the German government or the Greek

00:48:37.491 --> 00:48:44.890
government, the European Union, which operates
like a government, is not really a government.

00:48:44.890 --> 00:48:53.310
What is it? Come to think of it, it was created
in 1950 as a cartel. The first name of the

00:48:53.310 --> 00:48:58.150
European Union, let me remind you, was the
European Community of Coal and Steel. It was

00:48:58.150 --> 00:49:01.860
a clear cartel. And Brussels was the administrators
of the cartel.

00:49:01.860 --> 00:49:07.829
What does a cartel do? It sets and manipulates
prices, keeps them constant, stops antagonism

00:49:07.829 --> 00:49:13.890
and competition between the French coal producers
and the Germans and the Dutch, and then steel,

00:49:13.890 --> 00:49:18.480
and then automakers. Then they co-opted the
French farmers by means of the Common Agricultural

00:49:18.480 --> 00:49:22.560
Policy. It was a simple attempt to bribe them
to accept free trade on the basis that we

00:49:22.560 --> 00:49:29.010
will take a part of our oligopoly/cartel profits
and give them to you. And maybe I'm simplifying,

00:49:29.010 --> 00:49:35.800
but I don't think I'm simplifying too much.
And the problem with an administration whose

00:49:35.800 --> 00:49:44.550
raison d'�tre was to administer the cartel,
especially if you graft upon it a common currency

00:49:44.550 --> 00:49:49.690
and apolitical money which, of course, can
be political, because there's nothing more

00:49:49.690 --> 00:49:56.510
political than the apolitical. The combination
of a cartel administration and a monetary

00:49:56.510 --> 00:50:03.120
authority does not respond well, firstly,
to crisis. It is not-doesn't have the political

00:50:03.120 --> 00:50:11.150
legitimacy for handling the distribution of
burdens during the downturn, and secondly

00:50:11.150 --> 00:50:18.210
has a deep contempt for democracy. And I experienced
that. Anybody who has been inside the belly

00:50:18.210 --> 00:50:23.370
of the beast in Brussels or Frankfurt knows
that democracy is only something to be accepted

00:50:23.370 --> 00:50:31.630
in name, to be denied in substance.
And this is terrible because, why? It's highly

00:50:31.630 --> 00:50:36.710
problematic, because democracy is not a luxury.
The reason why we have democratic processes

00:50:36.710 --> 00:50:44.580
is because it is the only way of regulating
crisis, and regulating the fine balance between

00:50:44.580 --> 00:50:49.750
the economics and the political legitimacy
which is necessary to carry a population along

00:50:49.750 --> 00:50:54.250
the path of difficult reform and adjustment,
and so on and so forth. So Europe is absolutely

00:50:54.250 --> 00:50:59.460
incapable of doing that. And this is where
after my resignation I had a lot of pressure

00:50:59.460 --> 00:51:05.510
from people to create a new party. And I just
couldn't bring myself to do it. What was I

00:51:05.510 --> 00:51:10.070
to promise the Greek people? If they shoved
a microphone in my face, they would say, OK,

00:51:10.070 --> 00:51:15.430
so what do you-why do you-why should I vote
for you?

00:51:15.430 --> 00:51:20.570
And I would say the same things that I said
a year before when I was elected. And they

00:51:20.570 --> 00:51:26.290
would, rightly, ask me: So why didn't you
do it the first time around? We had a very

00:51:26.290 --> 00:51:32.350
small window of opportunity in the context
of the nation-state-of a small nation-state

00:51:32.350 --> 00:51:37.480
to affect-to reboot this extend and pretend.
Had we succeeded-I think we could have succeeded.

00:51:37.480 --> 00:51:42.651
It was not predetermined that we should fail.
We were divided, this is why we failed. Had

00:51:42.651 --> 00:51:47.060
we succeeded we might have rebooted the whole
process in Europe. But we failed. That opportunity

00:51:47.060 --> 00:51:50.750
was lost.
And then I started looking at Europe. I was-we

00:51:50.750 --> 00:51:56.930
were going to France, we were going to Germany.
And I could see genuine interest by the Germans,

00:51:56.930 --> 00:52:01.380
by the French, by the Italians, by the Spaniards,
by the British now, to have this conversation

00:52:01.380 --> 00:52:07.410
that Europe has always denied itself, a conversation
of what are the common threats that we're

00:52:07.410 --> 00:52:11.670
facing as Europeans, and what can we do about
it? It's a conversation we never had. The

00:52:11.670 --> 00:52:13.880
Five Presidents' Report does not deal with
that.

00:52:13.880 --> 00:52:19.580
You know, when the United States has a major
crisis-the United States is not perfect, right?

00:52:19.580 --> 00:52:24.540
But at least when you are facing an implosion
here, like you did in 2008, 10 people got

00:52:24.540 --> 00:52:29.690
around the table, oval table, square table,
and they asked the right question: How do

00:52:29.690 --> 00:52:34.570
we stop this crisis from consuming us? Do
you know what the Europeans ask themselves

00:52:34.570 --> 00:52:39.500
when there is such a crisis? How can we pretend
that the rules that we designed can still

00:52:39.500 --> 00:52:43.690
be applied? (Laughter.) It is not the same
question.

00:52:43.690 --> 00:52:48.370
When your policy is motivated by the second
question, you have a catastrophe. And the

00:52:48.370 --> 00:52:55.780
bad economic outcomes, because you've asked
this question, lead to terrible politics because

00:52:55.780 --> 00:53:00.220
you need increasing figures of authoritarianism,
and decreasing democracy, in order to keep

00:53:00.220 --> 00:53:08.250
applying them. And so there is this vicious
circle. So some of us, in a utopian twist,

00:53:08.250 --> 00:53:14.360
decided to put together a Pan-European democratic
movement, the purpose of which is to have

00:53:14.360 --> 00:53:19.580
this conversation. Just being by having this
conversation. We call it DIEM, as in carpe

00:53:19.580 --> 00:53:26.390
diem, Democracy in Europe Movement.
We have now, within two months, hundreds and

00:53:26.390 --> 00:53:30.000
thousands of branches that have spontaneously
emerged from Finland to Portugal, and from

00:53:30.000 --> 00:53:38.520
Britain all the way Turkey, actually, which
is very reassuring for me. And will it succeed?

00:53:38.520 --> 00:53:45.520
Probably not. But it motivates us to wake
up in the morning and do that which we think

00:53:45.520 --> 00:53:51.180
we must do as Europeans who fear that the
disintegration of Europe, this holy war between

00:53:51.180 --> 00:53:57.100
France and Germany; between east and west
now, Hungary and Poland on the one hand and

00:53:57.100 --> 00:54:03.280
Greece, Germany, and France on the other hand;
Britain and the continent-this disintegration,

00:54:03.280 --> 00:54:08.890
the re-erection of borders of electrified
fences between Austria and Germany, for God's

00:54:08.890 --> 00:54:14.650
sakes, this is creating a post-modern 1930s,
which is going-from an economic point of view

00:54:14.650 --> 00:54:21.190
going to create a deflationary shock in the
middle of a continent which is already sick

00:54:21.190 --> 00:54:26.170
financially. And this is not going to augur
well for the United States, for China, for

00:54:26.170 --> 00:54:31.730
Latin America. Europe has damaged the rest
of the world very significantly in the past.

00:54:31.730 --> 00:54:35.360
It has the capacity and the inanity to do
it again.

00:54:35.360 --> 00:54:39.650
So, Europe is too important to be left to
the Europeans-(laughter)-and certainly to

00:54:39.650 --> 00:54:44.620
its European five presidents and rulers in
Brussels and so on. In exactly the same way

00:54:44.620 --> 00:54:51.330
that the Americans in the 1940s with Bretton
Woods played a significant role in stabilizing

00:54:51.330 --> 00:54:57.140
the world by stabilizing Europe, I would very
much welcome participation of the United States

00:54:57.140 --> 00:55:03.620
it the stabilization and democratization of
Europe. And I lament the fact that the current

00:55:03.620 --> 00:55:10.190
administration, and a lot more so the future
administrations that seem to be coming out

00:55:10.190 --> 00:55:16.760
of the woodwork, are retreating from Europe.
SHEARD: Great. Time to turn to you, Benn.

00:55:16.760 --> 00:55:22.420
Q: Benn Steil, Council on Foreign Relations.
This has been an extremely stimulating conversation,

00:55:22.420 --> 00:55:28.601
but I'd like to keep you on the issue of looking
forward, and specifically at Greece. You've

00:55:28.601 --> 00:55:35.710
understandably condemned the strategy of extend
and pretend, but obviously the German government,

00:55:35.710 --> 00:55:42.110
Chancellor Merkel in particular, needs political
cover with her public to go forward. She can't

00:55:42.110 --> 00:55:46.330
just say, OK, it's time for us to acknowledge
we're never going to get paid back, here's

00:55:46.330 --> 00:55:54.940
the bill. Isn't there a way forward, perhaps,
with some empathy on both sides, for an extend-and-pretend

00:55:54.940 --> 00:56:02.410
plus? That is, extending debt maturities further;
extending debt holidays further; not only

00:56:02.410 --> 00:56:08.420
lowering interest rates to zero, but pegging
them to the ECB's deposit rate-that is, negative

00:56:08.420 --> 00:56:14.000
interest rates-as a way forward out of this
crisis. Again, with some goodwill, could we

00:56:14.000 --> 00:56:20.320
not do it that way?
VAROUFAKIS: Well, if you look at the-a policy

00:56:20.320 --> 00:56:25.880
paper which I submitted to the German government
and to the troika on the 11th of May last

00:56:25.880 --> 00:56:35.080
year, it was actually a lot less radical than
what you suggest. (Laughter.) There was no

00:56:35.080 --> 00:56:41.770
haircut, not even at the level of net present
value, involved. It was those debt swaps that

00:56:41.770 --> 00:56:48.260
I was referring to.
And it actually comprised three chapters.

00:56:48.260 --> 00:56:55.831
The first one was this sequence of debt swaps
that would make it possible for the Greek

00:56:55.831 --> 00:57:02.330
debt to be sustainable with a 1.5 percent
primary surplus ad infinitum-a target which

00:57:02.330 --> 00:57:10.550
is not too easy to achieve, but doable, and
would not involve a self-perpetuating cycle

00:57:10.550 --> 00:57:16.050
between austerity and recession, OK? So that
was chapter one.

00:57:16.050 --> 00:57:23.750
Chapter two was particular-you know, the medium-term
fiscal policy, which was designed in such

00:57:23.750 --> 00:57:34.440
a way as to consolidate and to ameliorate
for the five, six years of the depression.

00:57:34.440 --> 00:57:45.100
And the third part was the reforms. And we
were proposing really brutal reforms. So create-wrenching

00:57:45.100 --> 00:57:48.840
out of my ministry the whole of the public
revenues department and creating a genuinely

00:57:48.840 --> 00:57:58.320
autonomous, independent IRS modeled on the
American IRS. We had proposals for reforming

00:57:58.320 --> 00:58:04.220
the product markets, particularly breaking
down cartels, oligopolies.

00:58:04.220 --> 00:58:09.330
In Greece, for instance-let me just give you
one small example of what I mean. In the last

00:58:09.330 --> 00:58:15.750
six years, we've lost, as I said, 28 percent
of nominal GDP. Wages came down by 42 percent

00:58:15.750 --> 00:58:21.500
in nominal terms. You can imagine why that
is. Prices came down by 8 percent. Now, the

00:58:21.500 --> 00:58:27.090
only explanation of this is an increase in
concentration rate, increasing monopoly power,

00:58:27.090 --> 00:58:32.360
and cartelized product markets. So we were
talking about breaking those down.

00:58:32.360 --> 00:58:39.020
We identified as major reforms the creation
of a bad bank that would be partly funded

00:58:39.020 --> 00:58:45.560
by loans that we had already secured from
the creditors in order to create a market

00:58:45.560 --> 00:58:52.350
for non-performing loans, to cleanse a part
of the tranches of the NPLs that were impeding

00:58:52.350 --> 00:59:00.160
the provision of credit to the productive
and profitable firms. And finally, a development

00:59:00.160 --> 00:59:05.770
bank that-where we would transfer the remaining
public assets of the state to be managed at

00:59:05.770 --> 00:59:12.540
an arm's length from politics, but within
a public investment bank that would use the

00:59:12.540 --> 00:59:18.100
remaining public assets as collateral to create
a homegrown investment fund, which, in association

00:59:18.100 --> 00:59:23.680
with a European investment bank, would restart
the engine of investment growth. So this-and

00:59:23.680 --> 00:59:31.430
let me tell you, just so that you know, who
the co-authors were of this, because that,

00:59:31.430 --> 00:59:39.710
again, is-signals something, and it helps
ameliorate for this disconnect between the

00:59:39.710 --> 00:59:44.090
reality and what was being reported.
So, co-authors, some of them you may know.

00:59:44.090 --> 00:59:50.800
Jeffrey Sachs was my right-hand person. We
wrote this together in-we were working night

00:59:50.800 --> 00:59:59.250
and day. Somebody called Larry Summers; maybe
you've heard of him. (Laughter.) Lord Lamont,

00:59:59.250 --> 01:00:05.160
former chancellor of the exchequer of John
Major-not exactly a communist. And Thomas

01:00:05.160 --> 01:00:10.230
Mayer, the chief economist-the former chief
economist of Deutsche Bank.

01:00:10.230 --> 01:00:18.170
So we presented this to the troika and to
the German government. Now, it was very painful,

01:00:18.170 --> 01:00:23.640
not that they rejected it. They never rejected
it. You know why? They never discussed it

01:00:23.640 --> 01:00:31.920
with us. They refused us the right to discuss
it. The question is, why? But it's a long

01:00:31.920 --> 01:00:36.250
question, maybe for another meeting.
SHEARD: Thank you very much. I think that

01:00:36.250 --> 01:00:39.720
is for another meeting because we have roll
this particular one to a close. But I'd like

01:00:39.720 --> 01:00:50.080
to thank Yanis Varoufakis very much for his
presentation today. (Applause.) Thank you

01:00:50.080 --> 01:00:55.760
very much indeed.
(END)

